s  brains  bodies so we really do want to keep them  apart so let's talk first about sort of  these isolated expletives right words  that have been determined to be you know  a good framework in the u.s. to think  about is FCC regulations and sort of  words that are on a list which can't or  shouldn't be said if an if a program or  channel wants to avoid fines how do  those words sort of come about as words  that better considered bad words  historically how does it happen socially  how does it happen and is there some  element of sort of society giving these  words more power by putting them on  these arbitrary lists yeah that's  exactly how it works so if you look  across the world's languages most of  them have something like an idea that  there are certain words that are bad  that are taboo that are profane and  across the world's languages you find  that these words overwhelmingly tend to  come from one of four major sources they  tend to be drawn from words that at  their origin have religious meetings  that have meanings related to sex  meanings related to bodily functions in  general and then meetings related to  other social groups usually marginalized  social groups and specific ones of those  of those groups specific words from  those categories will come to be over  time decided upon by a social group as  the words that are the bad ones and so  you can think about for example lots of  words about sex that are absolutely  profane and that you would get fined for  for using on television or radio and  lots of other words that would be the  type of thing that you would use with  your therapist or your doctor or maybe  even with children when you're  explaining the birds and the bees so  it's not the it's not the meaning that  they had at their origin that is the  determiner that they will be ultimately  decided as as profane it's a contributor  what has to happen is exactly what you  said that people have to decide well  this particular word it makes me  uncomfortable it makes me feel like it  could cause harm to children or whatever  reason maybe the way that it sounds and  and as a result by implementing sort of  social control on the use of those words  you're right that censorship  is one type of social control but it  happens in in the home and in the  classroom as well when a child is  punished for using a word when the soap  comes out for example or when children  are told that you know this word is  really not appropriate for for school or  when parents react in a very strong  emotional way the child is learning that  this word is a powerful word it has  impact I can use it to control my  environment including my parents and  other adults and that's a really  exciting thing for a child and it's a  lesson that they learn implicitly that  then carries with them for their entire  life so the words that we now think of  as profane the words that now have  physiological impact on us as adults we  uh we only learned that they were  powerful because of those social  controls that were implemented early on  in our lives do we have researcher data  that tells us anything about the sort of  social impact of uh being in a in a  country for example where swearing is  just generally less of a big deal in my  personal sort of references that I'm  from Argentina and on television and  radio in Argentina swearing is just not  really a big deal even to some degree  nudity on television outside of the  so-called safe harbor hours that exist  here in the US it's not really a big  deal and I'd be interested to know if we  have any data about how this impacts and  it could be children it could be adults  or just any anybody yeah great question  well you're right that there's huge  variation from culture to culture in how  how strictly the regulations are imposed  on on specific language so on one end of  the scale you have places like like  Afghanistan where where lass famiy is a  capital offense on the other hand you  have something like so Argentina i think  is a pretty good example may be an even  more extreme example would be something  like france where even the strongest  words show up in the mouths of children  and the mouths of teachers and then the  mouths of the President of the Republic  on the floor of the Senate so there's  much less there's a much less strict  relationship to language in those  societies and I'd say that in the United  States were  somewhere in between probably closer to  France than Afghanistan in that we have  some social controls that we impose we  don't really know what the differences  are in the effects on on children so we  can't say for example that in  Afghanistan children are more aggressive  because profanity is stronger because  there are so many other variables across  cultures that go into affecting things  like childhood well-being their  psychological state their their physical  well-being and all that all that sort of  stuff so it's sort of an it's an  uncontrolled experiment we can't really  know but but we certainly know that for  people who speak languages where there  is more social control present speakers  of those languages experience strong or  physiological reactions to the profane  words of their language so we know that  there's something about those early  childhood and continuing experiences  what we're told these are bad words we  may engender really strong emotional  reactions and other people we  internalize that we we internalize that  into our bodies such that our our heart  rates increase our palm start sweating  and so on whenever we're exposed to one  of those words when we talk now about  slurs versus swears let's let's dig into  that a little bit I mean what what can  we say distinguishes the impact of maybe  not only the hearing of but also the  normalization of whether they be racial  ethnic religious gender etcetera slurs  uh it could be on children but also more  generally in society as as you sort of  alluded to at the beginning of the  interview that's a whole different ball  game isn't it of course yeah so there's  some good social psychology evidence  that even merely overhearing a slur even  unconsciously will affect the way that  you treat people in that marginalized  group so I can talk about a couple of  experiments in one people were  subconsciously presented with a  homophobic slur and in the control  condition they were presented again  unconsciously with the word homosexual  and after that they didn't know that  they had actually been exposed to this  word because it was it was displayed so  quickly on the screen  15 milliseconds faster than you can  consciously apprehend they were a get  been asked to perform some tasks one of  the tasks was they were told to go into  another room and to set up some chairs  for a conversation they were going to  have with another student at the  University who was a homosexual and and  and what the experimenters measured was  how far apart the participants put those  two chairs and what they found was that  people who had been primed with the  homophobic slur positioned their chairs  about 10 centimeters farther apart than  the people who had been primed with the  word homosexual so there's something  about this slur that's physically  driving people apart it's leading people  to not want to be close to homosexuals  in this particular case you also see  economic discrimination so people again  who are subconsciously exposed to these  homophobic slurs decide that when when  asked how resources should be allocated  at the level of the government decide  that less money should go to AIDS and  HIV prevention than people who were  primed with with the word homosexual so  there's a lot of there's a lot of  downstream consequences for merely  overhearing or overseeing these these  words and we also know that at least in  correlational studies kids who are  called by slurs again mostly homophobic  slurs have been studied um display the  greatest decreases over the course of  Middle School in their emotional  well-being so the kids who report being  called things like homo and lesbo and  and slurs like that also report that  over the course of 6th and 7th and 8th  grades they they show that they have a  greatest increase in depression anxiety  and feelings of detachment from their  schools so there's a lot of sort of  convergent evidence that slurs can be  harmful now that's not to say that they  always are slurs can be used in positive  ways as well and a lot of them get  reappropriation groups who are  marginalized by them but that's sort of  a more complicated wrinkle well I think  it will not come as a surprise to many  of our progressive viewers that the  focus of the religious right on isolated  words that shouldn't be said  versus real ways in which people are  sort of being insulted uh may not be the  right focus if we're trying to increase  well-being overall I think many in our  audience will will say well yes David uh  we've been speaking about the book what  the f what swearing reveals about our  language our brains in ourselves we've  been speaking with the book's author  professor ben bergen thank you so much  for talking to us about this it's my  pleasure thanks so much
 

